# BioTron deployment architecture

- Run the same topology on personal staging and Oracle production: Cloudflare DNS/edge -> dedicated Cloudflare Tunnel -> `cloudflared` container -> Nginx container -> application containers -> one Postgres + one Redis per environment.
- Cloudflare Tunnel is the only public ingress. Tailscale SSH is the only administrative path. Do not expose SSH, Nginx, application, Postgres, or Redis ports publicly.
- Use two Docker networks: `edge` contains only `cloudflared` and Nginx; `biotron` contains Nginx, applications, Postgres, and Redis. Nginx is the only bridge.
- Use one application per hostname consistently: `www`, `calendar`, `auth`, `exo`, `logs`, and `sprinter` under the environment domain. The apex may redirect to `www`. BioTronStyle has no production runtime; a staging preview is optional.
- Within each application hostname, route `/` to its frontend and `/api/` to its backend. Calendar feeds remain on the calendar hostname. Prefer relative browser API URLs to compiled localhost or cross-origin URLs.
- OAuthManager provides redirect-based SSO. Each application and OAuthManager use separate `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, host-only session cookies; never set `Domain` to the parent BioTron domain. An app without a local session redirects through OAuthManager, which reuses its own session, checks that app's `read`/`write`/`admin` grant, and returns without another GitHub prompt.
- Staging uses `*.biotron-dev.com`; production uses the same hostname layout under its production domain. Domains, secrets, data, capacity, and release approval differ; routing and containers do not.
- Build multi-architecture (`linux/amd64`, `linux/arm64`) images once in CI, tag/pin them by immutable digest, exercise the candidate in staging, and promote the exact same digests to production. Never rebuild a production candidate after staging approval.
- `Server` owns edge configuration, shared infrastructure, pinned deployment manifests, startup, backups, and recovery. Product repos own their application images and local development Compose files.
